        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Laminate Flooring Picks",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-laminate-flooring/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which laminate line is the safest all-round buy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homes, an AC4 water-resistant line in the 8 to 12 mm range with an attached pad is the safest buy. Pergo Outlast+ balances price, spill protection and availability. Mohawk RevWood Plus is the pick for heavy spill exposure, Quick-Step NatureTEK Plus for realism, and TrafficMaster for low-traffic rooms on a tight budget."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need AC4 or is AC3 enough?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "AC3 handles bedrooms, guest rooms and living spaces with modest traffic. Choose AC4 for kitchens, hallways, entries and homes with active kids or large dogs. The price gap is usually small, and the harder surface is the cheapest way to extend the life of a busy floor. AC5 is commercial grade and rarely needed at home."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can laminate survive a spill or leak?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Modern water-resistant laminate tolerates topical spills for a warranted window, often 24 to 72 hours depending on the line. It will not survive a flood, an unnoticed plumbing leak or water rising from a slab, and warranties exclude those. Wipe spills promptly and use vinyl plank in full baths, laundry rooms and damp basements."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a thicker laminate board better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Thicker boards, 10 to 12 mm, feel more solid, sound less hollow and bridge small subfloor dips better. They are not more scratch resistant; that comes from the AC rating. In open living spaces and upstairs rooms, the extra thickness is worth paying for. In bedrooms and closets, 8 mm with a good underlayment is enough."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is laminate a realistic DIY project?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for a square room with a sound subfloor. You need a fine-tooth saw, a tapping block, pull bar, spacers and patience with the first rows. Budget extra time for subfloor prep, undercutting door casings and transitions. Most first-timers manage a bedroom in a day and a large living room over a weekend."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "When is vinyl plank the better call?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Choose vinyl plank for full bathrooms, laundry rooms, basements on concrete, and any room where water can sit or rise from below, because its core cannot swell. Choose laminate for dry living spaces, bedrooms and hallways where scratch resistance and a wood-like feel matter most. Many homes use both, split by room."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What should I clean laminate with?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sweep or vacuum on a hard-floor setting, then use a barely damp microfiber mop with a cleaner approved by the manufacturer. Avoid steam mops, soaking-wet mops, oil soaps, wax and polishes, which leave film or push moisture into joints. Wipe spills dry immediately, and keep felt pads and door mats in good shape."
          }
