              <a href="#install" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Laying It Yourself</span></a>
              <a href="#versus-vinyl" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Laminate or Vinyl Plank</span></a>
              <a href="#budget" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Price and Budget</span></a>
              <a href="#looks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Color and Finish</span></a>
              <a href="#upkeep" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Upkeep and Repairs</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">13.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Laminate earns its place on one fact: its melamine top surface is harder than the urethane on vinyl plank or site-finished wood, so dog claws, kids' toys and grit leave fewer marks. Its weakness is the fiberboard core, which swells if water gets into it and stays. The current generation of sealed-edge, water-resistant laminate has narrowed that gap a great deal. Pick a line rated for your traffic, protect the core, and laminate is one of the best values in flooring.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 7 Best Laminate Floors, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Picks are ranked on published abrasion class, plank thickness, core and edge treatment, the length and terms of each spill or water warranty, availability, and recurring owner feedback about chipping, swelling and joint wear. Nothing here was installed or trialed for this list; specifications come from manufacturer data sheets and retailer listings and vary by collection.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="ac-ratings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Abrasion Ratings Are the First Filter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate's toughness is graded by the European EN 13329 standard, which assigns an <strong>AC rating</strong> from AC1 to AC5 based on abrasion, impact, staining, burns and swelling. The higher the class, the more traffic the surface is built to absorb. Most packaging prints the AC class on the end of the box, and if a product does not list one at all, treat that as a warning sign rather than an oversight.
          </p>

          <p>
            AC1 and AC2 are for bedrooms and closets that see socks more than shoes, and you will rarely find them in stores now. AC3 is the common entry rating and is fine through most of a house with ordinary traffic. AC4 handles kitchens, hallways, entries and active households with pets, and it is also rated for light commercial use such as small offices. AC5 is built for heavy commercial traffic and is more than most homes need.
          </p>

          <p>
            The jump from AC3 to AC4 is the one worth paying for in a family home. It usually costs well under a dollar more per square foot, and it buys a harder surface in the rooms where grit and chair legs do the most damage. Some brands also grade themselves on in-house scales or quote Taber abrasion cycles; when you see those, compare them only within that brand's own lineup.
          </p>

          <p>
            A high AC rating does not make laminate scratch-proof. Sand tracked in on shoes is harder than any melamine coating and will dull a floor over time. Mats at doors and felt pads under furniture matter just as much as the rating on the carton. Think of AC class as how long the floor can take abuse, not whether it can be abused.
          </p>

