        <div id="thickness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Thickness and Core: What Actually Changes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate ranges from about 6 mm to 14 mm, with 7, 8, 10 and 12 mm the common sizes. Thickness does not change scratch resistance, which comes from the top layer. What it changes is feel and sound. Thicker boards are stiffer, so they sound less hollow when you walk on them, they bridge small subfloor dips better, and their deeper profile allows more pronounced beveled edges that read more like real wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 12 mm board is worth the extra cost in long, open rooms and on upper floors where footfall noise travels below. An 8 mm board is fine for bedrooms and smaller spaces, especially with a quality underlayment. Very thin 6 to 7 mm products are budget picks that can sound tinny and are less forgiving of an imperfect subfloor, so save them for rentals, closets or quick flips.
          </p>

          <p>
            The core itself is high-density fiberboard. Density and the resin treatment of the core affect how it responds to moisture and how well the locking joints hold. Premium lines use denser boards and treat the core and edges with water-repelling coatings or wax, which is how they achieve their water-resistance claims. You cannot see this on the shelf, which is why the warranty language on water is a useful proxy for core quality.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look closely at the locking profile too. Robust angle-angle locks with a solid tongue resist chipping during install and stay tight longer. Owners commonly report that cheap boards chip at the corners during installation and that joints open slightly in winter, both of which point to softer cores and weaker locks.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SHORTHAND</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">AC rating sets wear; thickness sets feel.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your budget is tight, keep the AC4 rating and drop to a thinner board with a good underlayment, rather than buying a thick AC3 board for a busy room.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="water-claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Spill and Water Claims, Decoded
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate marketing now uses three tiers of language. Standard laminate has no water protection beyond a quick wipe. <strong>Water-resistant</strong> laminate seals the edges and treats the core so that topical spills can sit for a stated period, often 24 to 72 hours, without swelling. The strongest claims, often labeled waterproof, cover topical water on the surface for the life of the warranty under specific conditions. None of these is the same as a floor you can flood.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read the warranty for three details: how long standing water is covered, whether the floor must be installed with specific perimeter sealing or underlayment, and what is excluded. Nearly all exclude flooding, plumbing leaks that run unnoticed, wet mopping and steam mops. If your goal is a laundry room or full bath that may see real water, vinyl plank is still the safer core.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-resistant lines change where laminate can go. A kitchen that sees occasional spills, a powder room without a tub and a mudroom with wet boots are now realistic laminate rooms, provided you wipe water up the same day. Owners commonly report that modern sealed-edge boards survive dog bowl splashes and dropped ice that would have swollen older laminate within hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weak point is no longer the board but the perimeter and transitions. Water that runs off a dishwasher or under a door threshold into the expansion gap reaches the edge of the boards and the subfloor. In wet-prone areas, seal the perimeter and transitions with the sealant the manufacturer specifies, and leave expansion gaps clean so the caulk can flex.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rooms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where Laminate Belongs, and Where It Does Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Living rooms, dining rooms, bedrooms and hallways</strong> are laminate's home ground. These rooms see shoes, furniture and pets, but rarely standing water, and laminate's hard surface keeps them looking new for longer than most vinyl at the same price. Choose AC4 for halls and family rooms and AC3 for bedrooms if you want to save.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Kitchens</strong> are fine with a water-resistant AC4 line. Leave the expansion gap at cabinets, run the floor under appliances where the instructions allow, and lay a mat at the sink. Install a leak sensor under the dishwasher and fridge water line, because a slow leak is what ruins laminate kitchens, not a dropped glass.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Full bathrooms, laundry rooms and below-grade basements</strong> are better served by vinyl. A tub overflow or a washer hose failure delivers more water than any laminate warranty covers, and basements combine moisture from the slab with the risk of a seep. If a basement is consistently dry and conditioned, a water-resistant laminate over a proper vapor barrier can work, but our <a href="/best-vinyl-plank-flooring/">vinyl plank picks</a> are the lower-risk choice there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stairs are possible with laminate using matching stair nose pieces, but it is slow, fussy work and the nose trim can feel slippery. Sunrooms and three-season porches with big temperature swings are a poor fit, since laminate needs a stable climate. Check each product's rules for unconditioned spaces before buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="underlayment" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Underlayment, Vapor Barriers and Slabs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate floats on a thin cushion that smooths tiny subfloor flaws, quiets footsteps and, on concrete, blocks moisture. Many boards come with a pad already attached. If yours does, do not add another foam layer, because a double pad lets the joints flex and break. On concrete, you still lay a 6 mil poly vapor barrier under an attached pad unless the manufacturer says the pad includes one.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the boards have no pad, choose an underlayment that matches the subfloor. Over plywood or OSB, a basic foam or a denser acoustic pad works. Over concrete, use a combination pad with a built-in vapor barrier, overlap and tape the seams, and run it slightly up the wall under the baseboard. In condos, check the building's sound rating requirements, which often specify a minimum IIC rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before laying anything on a slab, test it. Tape a square of plastic sheeting to the floor for a day or two and look for condensation underneath. A damp result means the slab has a moisture source that needs fixing first, whether that is grading, gutters or a crack. Running a <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier</a> helps control air humidity, but it will not stop water wicking up through concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flatness tolerances are similar to vinyl plank, usually about three-sixteenths of an inch over 10 feet. Laminate is stiffer than vinyl, so it rides over small high spots, but dips cause boards to flex at the joints and eventually creak or separate. Fix the subfloor with patch and a grinder or sander before you start.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Common Install Specs</p>
