        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Laying Laminate Yourself
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate is a reasonable first flooring project if the room is square and the subfloor is sound. You need a miter saw or circular saw for crosscuts, a jigsaw for notches, a tapping block and pull bar, spacers, a rubber mallet, a square, and a jamb saw for undercutting door casings. Unlike rigid vinyl, laminate does not score and snap cleanly, so a saw with a fine-tooth blade is essential. Cut outdoors or with dust collection, as fiberboard dust is fine and messy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acclimate the unopened cartons flat in the room for the time the maker specifies, often 48 hours. Plan rows so the last one is at least a few inches wide, stagger end joints by 8 inches or more, and mix boards from several cartons. Laminate prints repeat, so set aside obvious duplicates and spread them out across the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Laminate expands more than vinyl, so the perimeter gap is usually larger, often three-eighths of an inch, and transitions are required at doorways and long runs per the instructions. Do not trap the floor under cabinets, door jambs or heavy built-ins. Undercut the casing and slide the boards under it so the trim covers the edge without pinching it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tap gently. Laminate tongues chip if you drive them with a hammer directly, so always use the tapping block, and use the pull bar for the last board against the wall. If a joint shows a hairline gap, unclick and reset it rather than forcing it closed; a poorly seated joint is where water and wear start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finish with baseboard or quarter-round nailed to the wall, never to the floor. Install transition strips, reattach door stops and put felt pads on every furniture leg before moving pieces back. A competent DIYer typically lays a few hundred square feet over a weekend once the subfloor prep is done.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="versus-vinyl" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Laminate or Vinyl Plank
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate beats vinyl plank on scratch resistance, realism and feel. Its embossed surfaces track the printed grain closely, the boards sound more like wood, and the top layer shrugs off pet claws better than a similarly priced vinyl wear layer. If the space is dry and sees heavy traffic from dogs and kids, a good AC4 laminate usually looks better for longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vinyl plank beats laminate wherever water can sit or come from below. Its core cannot swell, so it is the safer choice for full baths, laundry rooms and basements. It is also quieter on concrete and more tolerant of rooms that are not kept at a steady temperature. Our <a href="/best-vinyl-plank-flooring/">vinyl plank flooring guide</a> lays out which cores and wear layers suit those rooms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prices overlap so much that cost rarely decides the question. Decide by room. Many households run laminate in living spaces and bedrooms and vinyl in wet rooms, joined with a T-molding at the doorway. If you want one floor for an entire open plan that includes a kitchen, a water-resistant AC4 laminate or a 20 mil vinyl both work, so choose based on the look and feel you prefer.
          </p>

