        <div id="budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Price and Budget
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, entry-level AC3 laminate starts around $1 to $2 per square foot, mid-range AC4 boards with water resistance run roughly $2 to $4, and premium 12 mm lines with long spill warranties and realistic embossing reach $4 to $6. Big-box store brands frequently go on sale, so time a large order around a promotion if you can.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add 10 percent for waste in a simple room, 15 percent in rooms with many corners or angled walls. Underlayment, if the boards have none attached, runs roughly $0.30 to $1 per square foot, and transitions, quarter-round and sealant add a few hundred dollars to a typical room. Professional installation typically runs $2 to $5 per square foot, more with subfloor prep, removal of old flooring or stairs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best value is usually an AC4 water-resistant board in the 8 to 10 mm range with an attached pad. It covers the busiest rooms of a typical house, avoids a separate underlayment purchase on wood subfloors, and is easy to find. Spending more buys thickness and realism, not durability. Spending less mainly buys you a softer core and a shorter useful life in busy rooms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="looks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Color, Width and Finish Choices
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate is a photograph under a clear wear surface, so the choices that make it look real are about texture and variation as much as color. Look for embossed-in-register texture, where the raised grain lines up with the printed grain. Run your hand across a sample: if you feel the knots and grain where you see them, the floor will hold up in raking light from a window. Flat prints with a generic texture look convincing only from a distance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mid-tone browns and gray-beiges hide dust, pet hair and fine scratches best. Very dark floors show every footprint and paw print, and very light whitewashed floors show dirt and grit at the doorway. If you have a dog that sheds, hold a sample next to the fur color, because a floor that matches the dog hides an extra day between vacuums.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wider and longer boards, 7 inches or more and 50 inches or longer, make a room look calmer and show fewer seams, which suits large open plans. Narrow boards suit smaller rooms and traditional houses. Check the number of unique board images in the line; budget products may repeat every few boards, which creates obvious patterns unless you shuffle cartons carefully during install.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sheen matters more than most buyers expect. Low-gloss and matte finishes hide scratches and scuffs far better than high-gloss laminate, which shows every mark in sunlight. Beveled edges, whether painted or pressed, help each board read as a separate plank and also hide slight height differences between boards. Order physical samples and look at them in the actual room at different times of day before committing to hundreds of square feet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Upkeep and Repairs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Keep grit off it. Sweep or vacuum with a hard-floor setting, and use a microfiber mop that is barely damp. Never wet-mop or use a steam mop, because liquid driven into joints is what swells laminate cores. Use cleaners approved by the maker; oil soaps, waxes and polishes leave a dull film that attracts dirt and cannot be buffed out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Felt pads under furniture, a chair mat under rolling office chairs, and mats at exterior doors cut most surface wear. Trim pets' nails regularly; even AC4 laminate shows fine scratches where large dogs turn and skid. Lift, do not drag, heavy furniture, and put appliances on a sheet of hardboard when you slide them in or out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small chips and scratches can be filled with color-matched laminate repair putty or wax sticks sold by most flooring brands. A swollen or badly damaged board near a wall can be unclicked and replaced row by row. In the middle of the room, a professional can cut out the board and glue in a replacement with the locking lip trimmed off. Keep a carton from the original order for repairs, because later production runs rarely match exactly.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a board starts to peak or the joints open with the seasons, check for pinned edges at the walls or cabinets and look for humidity swings. Holding indoor humidity steady, roughly 35 to 55 percent, keeps laminate movement within what the joints can absorb.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a budget AC3 board in the kitchen</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Entry-level laminate is fine in bedrooms, but kitchens, entries and pet-heavy rooms wear through it much faster. Pay the small premium for AC4 water-resistant boards in those spaces, and save the cheaper line for rooms that see mostly socks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trapping the floor at the edges</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Laminate expands and contracts with humidity. A missing gap at walls, door jambs or cabinets, or trim nailed into the boards, pins the floor so it buckles or opens up at the joints. Keep the full gap, undercut door casings and nail trim to the wall only.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the vapor barrier on concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A slab releases moisture constantly, even when it looks dry. Without a 6 mil poly barrier or a combination pad, that vapor works into the fiberboard core and swells the board edges. Test the slab, lay the barrier with taped seams, and run it slightly up the wall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mopping with a wet mop or steam</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Laminate's core is wood fiber. Water forced into joints by a soaked mop or steam mop swells the edges over time, even on water-resistant lines. Use a barely damp microfiber pad and a cleaner the manufacturer approves, and wipe spills dry the same day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doubling up on underlayment</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Boards with an attached pad are engineered to sit directly on the subfloor or a thin poly barrier. Adding a foam pad under them makes the floor too soft, so the joints flex and break. Read the box: if a pad is attached, add only a vapor barrier on slabs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which laminate line is the safest all-round buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, an AC4 water-resistant line in the 8 to 12 mm range with an attached pad is the safest buy. Pergo Outlast+ balances price, spill protection and availability. Mohawk RevWood Plus is the pick for heavy spill exposure, Quick-Step NatureTEK Plus for realism, and TrafficMaster for low-traffic rooms on a tight budget.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need AC4 or is AC3 enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                AC3 handles bedrooms, guest rooms and living spaces with modest traffic. Choose AC4 for kitchens, hallways, entries and homes with active kids or large dogs. The price gap is usually small, and the harder surface is the cheapest way to extend the life of a busy floor. AC5 is commercial grade and rarely needed at home.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can laminate survive a spill or leak?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Modern water-resistant laminate tolerates topical spills for a warranted window, often 24 to 72 hours depending on the line. It will not survive a flood, an unnoticed plumbing leak or water rising from a slab, and warranties exclude those. Wipe spills promptly and use vinyl plank in full baths, laundry rooms and damp basements.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a thicker laminate board better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Thicker boards, 10 to 12 mm, feel more solid, sound less hollow and bridge small subfloor dips better. They are not more scratch resistant; that comes from the AC rating. In open living spaces and upstairs rooms, the extra thickness is worth paying for. In bedrooms and closets, 8 mm with a good underlayment is enough.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is laminate a realistic DIY project?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for a square room with a sound subfloor. You need a fine-tooth saw, a tapping block, pull bar, spacers and patience with the first rows. Budget extra time for subfloor prep, undercutting door casings and transitions. Most first-timers manage a bedroom in a day and a large living room over a weekend.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is vinyl plank the better call?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose vinyl plank for full bathrooms, laundry rooms, basements on concrete, and any room where water can sit or rise from below, because its core cannot swell. Choose laminate for dry living spaces, bedrooms and hallways where scratch resistance and a wood-like feel matter most. Many homes use both, split by room.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I clean laminate with?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sweep or vacuum on a hard-floor setting, then use a barely damp microfiber mop with a cleaner approved by the manufacturer. Avoid steam mops, soaking-wet mops, oil soaps, wax and polishes, which leave film or push moisture into joints. Wipe spills dry immediately, and keep felt pads and door mats in good shape.
              </p>
